@page "/agent-required"
@inject CardCaptureOptions Capture

<PageTitle>Card reader setup &middot; DI VMS</PageTitle>

<h1>This PC cannot read the card yet</h1>
<p class="page-sub">One component has to be installed on this machine</p>

<section class="card">
    <div class="prose">
        <p>
            The Emirates ID is read from the chip in the card, by the reader plugged into
            <strong>this</strong> PC. The visitor management system runs on a server, so it
            cannot reach that reader by itself: ICP's agent has to be installed here to do it.
        </p>

        @* The reason this page exists at all: eidatoolkit.js, left to itself, offers a Java
           download when no agent answers. An attendant mid-check-in should get an
           explanation and a person to call, not a JNLP file. *@
        <h2>What needs installing</h2>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>ICAToolkitService.msi</strong> — ICP's ID Card Toolkit agent, from the toolkit v3.1.6 bundle.</li>
            <li>The <strong>driver for the card reader</strong>, if Windows has not already found it. An ACS ACR39U works with the driver Windows supplies.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Both need an administrator, so this is a job for IT rather than for the desk.</p>

        <h2>If it is already installed</h2>
        <p>Then it is not answering. Worth checking, in this order:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>The <strong>EIDA Toolkit</strong> service is running in Services.</li>
            <li>Nothing else is on ports <strong>9004</strong>, <strong>9005</strong> or <strong>9020</strong>.</li>
            <li>
                The agent's certificate is trusted on this PC. The page reaches the agent at
                <code>@(Capture.Agent.HostName is { Length: > 0 } host ? host : (Capture.Agent.TlsEnabled ? "toolkitagent.emiratesid.ae" : "127.0.0.1"))</code>
                over @(Capture.Agent.TlsEnabled ? "a secure WebSocket (wss://)" : "a plain WebSocket (ws://)"), and a
                certificate the browser will not accept fails in a way that looks exactly like an agent that is not there.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Meanwhile</h2>
        <p>
            A visitor should not wait for this. Go back to
            <a href="">New Visitor</a> and use <strong>Enter manually</strong> — the entry
            is recorded and marked as entered by hand, so it is never mistaken for a chip read.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
